Ran `crimes context src/Card.tsx` in the fixture. Context beyond the scan JSON:

- **0 importers, low churn** (2 commits/90d, 1 author, last touched ~2 months ago) — nothing depends on this file, so all three fixes are low-blast-radius.
- **`test_gap: 1` on all three findings** and *"no sibling, `__tests__`, `.test`, `.spec` files matched"* — there is no test to catch a regression, which is why the medium a11y finding carries `agent_risk 0.44` despite `blast_radius 0`.
- `tailwind.config.ts` **does** define a token scale (`brand`, `surface`, `ink`) — so `design_token_escape` is a real escape from an existing system, not a missing one.

## What the file actually does today

Reading `src/Card.tsx` turns up three concrete defects the detectors point at but don't spell out:

1. `width: 800` and `maxWidth: 1024` (lines 12, 14) both apply — the `maxWidth` **never binds**, it's dead style.
2. Inner regions are `720px` + `240px` = **960px inside an 800px card** with 24px padding (752px usable). The card overflows at *every* viewport, not just small ones.
3. `outlineColor: "#1a73e8"` is Google blue, not the `brand` token `#0050ff` — the focus ring has already drifted off-palette.

## Proposed refactor

**Step 1 — `tailwind.config.ts`: name the tokens the file is reaching past** (prerequisite for `crime_00002`)

```ts
export default {
  theme: {
    colors: {
      brand: "#0050ff",
      surface: "#f7f7fa",
      ink: "#111111",
      "ink-muted": "#333333",   // was raw #333333 (line 24)
      "ink-subtle": "#666666",  // was raw #666666 (line 27)
      border: "#cccccc",        // was raw #cccccc (line 18)
    },
    extend: {
      boxShadow: { card: "0 2px 4px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.2)" },
    },
  },
};
```

**Step 2 — `src/Card.tsx`**

```tsx
import type { ReactNode } from "react";

type CardProps = {
  children: ReactNode;
  aside?: ReactNode;
  /** Primary action. Omit to render a non-interactive card. */
  action?: { label: string; onSelect: () => void };
};

export function Card({ children, aside, action }: CardProps) {
  return (
    <div className="@container relative w-full max-w-3xl rounded-lg border border-border bg-surface p-6 text-ink shadow-card">
      <div className="grid gap-6 @lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_15rem]">
        <div className="min-w-0 text-lg text-ink-muted">{children}</div>
        {aside ? (
          <div className="min-w-0 text-sm text-ink-subtle">{aside}</div>
        ) : null}
      </div>

      {action ? (
        <button
          type="button"
          onClick={action.onSelect}
          className="mt-4 rounded after:absolute after:inset-0 focus-visible:outline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-brand"
        >
          {action.label}
        </button>
      ) : null}
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Each change tied to its finding

**`crime_00001` — Accessible Interaction Risk** (medium, conf 0.85, `effort: small`)
Evidence: *"line 10: `<div>` with onClick has no role / aria-label / tabIndex."* The suggested action offers `role="button"` + `tabIndex={0}` as an alternative, but that's the wrong branch here: the clickable div **wraps `{children}`**, so adding `role="button"` would either swallow any interactive children into a button's accessible name or produce nested interactive content. The fix that matches `fix_shape` (*"use semantic elements; add ARIA only when semantics don't fit"*) is a real `<button type="button">` for the action, with `after:absolute after:inset-0` on a `relative` parent — the "stretched link" pattern. Full-card hit area preserved, but now with native focus, Enter/Space activation, and an accessible name from `action.label`. The `onClick={() => console.log("clicked")}` also becomes a real `onSelect` prop; a console.log handler is a placeholder that no keyboard user could reach anyway. `focus-visible:outline-brand` restores a visible focus ring using the token, replacing the drifted `#1a73e8`.

**`crime_00002` — Design Token Escape** (low, conf 0.75, `effort: quick`)
Evidence: 9 raw values across `color`/`px`/`rgb`/`shadow`. Dropping the `style` object for utility classes routes every one through the theme: `#0a0a0a`→`text-ink`, `#cccccc`→`border-border`, `#333333`→`text-ink-muted`, `#666666`→`text-ink-subtle`, `#1a73e8`→`outline-brand`, `rgb(0, 0, 0)`→`shadow-card`, `padding: 24`→`p-6`. Two need a decision rather than a mechanical swap:

- `background: "#ff00aa"` is magenta and near nothing in the palette. **I've assumed it should be `surface`** — the plausible reading is debug/placeholder color that shipped. Confirm before merging; this is the one change with product intent behind it.
- `boxShadow: "0 2px 4px rgb(0, 0, 0)"` is **fully opaque black**, almost certainly a missing alpha. The `shadow-card` token above adds `/ 0.2`. That's a visual change, deliberately — flagging rather than smuggling it in.

This is the finding whose summary names the agent risk directly: *"agents extending the file may copy them forward."* One raw hex in a `style` prop is a template the next agent clones.

**`crime_00003` — Responsive Fragility** (low, conf 0.65, `effort: small`)
Evidence: `width 800px`, `width 720px`, `fontSize 18px`, *"no `@media` query found in the file."* The `fix_shape` says *"drive layout from container queries or a fluid scale, not pixel media queries"* — so no `@media` is added at all:

- `width: 800` + dead `maxWidth: 1024` → `w-full max-w-3xl` (48rem cap, shrinks below it) — this is the `max-width` + `width: 100%` shape the suggested action asks for, and it kills the dead declaration.
- Fixed `720`/`240` children → `grid` with `@lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_15rem]`, single column below the breakpoint. `minmax(0,1fr)` + `min-w-0` stops grid-blowout. This fixes the 960-in-800 overflow as a side effect.
- `height: 600` → dropped. A fixed height on a children-driven container clips or leaves dead space; content should size it.
- `fontSize: 18/14` → `text-lg`/`text-sm` (rem). px font sizes ignore the user's browser font-size setting — an accessibility consequence, but correctly charged here since the detector flags it as fixed sizing.
- Container queries (`@container`/`@lg:`) mean the card responds to *its own* width, so it survives being dropped into a narrow column — a viewport media query wouldn't.

## Sequencing and verification

Do **a11y first** (it changes the element tree), then the style-object rewrite in one pass — tokens and responsive units land in the same edit, since both are deleting the same `style` object. Splitting them would mean touching every attribute twice.

Two things I'd add that the findings imply but don't state:

- **A test.** All three carry `test_gap: 1` and `crimes context` found no test file. One RTL test — tab to the action, press Enter, assert `onSelect` fired — locks in `crime_00001` permanently. Without it, the next agent editing this file re-opens the finding.
- Re-run `crimes scan --json` on the fixture; the file should drop to zero findings. If `design_token_escape` still fires, a raw value survived in an arbitrary-value class (`bg-[#...]`), which is the same escape wearing a Tailwind costume.

Two caveats: `@container`/`@lg:` needs `@tailwindcss/container-queries` on Tailwind v3 (built in on v4). And I parameterized the hardcoded `"sidebar"` string into an `aside` prop — that's a judgment call beyond the three findings; keep the literal if it's meant to be fixed chrome.

I have **not** edited the fixture — `07-stress-frontend` exists to keep these detectors firing, so applying this would zero out its finding set and move the eval baseline.
